<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="run-an-action"
      updated="August 18, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"before", "Before you dispatch"},
        {"select", "1. Select a runner and action"},
        {"arguments", "2. Enter arguments and reason"},
        {"dispatch", "3. Dispatch and approve"},
        {"result", "4. Read the result"},
        {"retry", "Before you retry"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="run-an-action">
        <:dek>
          Dispatch one declared action from the console. Review the action contract, enter its
          arguments and reason, pass policy, and read the recorded result.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="before">Before you dispatch</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>Your role is Operator, Admin, or Owner.</:item>
        <:item>The target runner is online and enabled.</:item>
        <:item>The runner advertises the action and its required program.</:item>
        <:item>The exact pack version and hash are trusted.</:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Signed-only runners use an MCP client">
        <p>
          The console does not sign dispatches. If the runner requires signatures, run the action
          from a configured MCP bridge. See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Signed dispatch</.link>.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="select">1. Select a runner and action</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Open <a
            href={~p"/app/runners"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Runners</a>.
        </li>
        <li>Select the runner that owns the target service or host.</li>
        <li>Find the action in its action catalog.</li>
        <li>Review the risk, title, and pack.</li>
        <li>Select <strong>Run</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A disabled Run button shows what is blocking it. Common causes include an offline runner, an
        untrusted pack, a missing program, or signed-only dispatch.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="arguments">2. Enter arguments and reason</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Read the action summary and every listed side effect, then fill each required
        argument — the form enforces the pack's types, choices, sizes, and path limits.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Enter a short, specific reason — Audit records it. Describe the goal, not a generic
        label such as <em>maintenance</em>.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Example reason">Confirm database health after the primary failover.</.docs_code>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/run-form.webp"
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
        title="emisar console · Run an action"
        caption="The action's contract sits above the form: summary, side effects, clickable examples, and risk — then each declared argument and the required reason."
        alt="The Run linux.grep_log page on runner edge-fra-01: the action summary, its side-effects list, two clickable JSON examples, the low risk, exec kind, and linux-core pack facts, then the file, pattern, and max_lines argument fields, the reason box noting it is logged to the audit trail, and the Dispatch to edge-fra-01 button."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="dispatch">3. Dispatch and approve</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Select <strong>Dispatch to &lt;runner&gt;</strong>. High-risk and critical actions show a final
        confirmation with the target and arguments.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Policy now returns Allow, Require approval, or Deny. A denied run stops immediately,
        and a run that needs approval waits on
        <a
          href={~p"/app/approvals"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Approvals</a>
        — the runner receives the action only after someone approves.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The approver sees the action, runner, arguments, reason, evidence, and expected
        result. If the pack, target, or a policy fact changed since the request, the
        approval is refused.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="result">4. Read the result</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        After dispatch, emisar opens the run page. It shows status, output, arguments, executed
        command, policy facts, actor, timestamps, and exit code.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li>Wait for a terminal status.</li>
        <li>Read both standard output and standard error.</li>
        <li>Check the exit code and any structured result.</li>
        <li>Make sure the result matches the reason for the run.</li>
        <li>
          Open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>
          when you need the full event trail.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        See
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runs"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Runs and history
        </.link>
        for status meanings, output limits, cancellation, and grouped operations.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="retry">Before you retry</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="An unknown result is not a failed action">
        <p>
          Check the target system before you retry an unknown result. The action can finish after a
          runner or network failure hides its final response. emisar never repeats it automatically.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Fix denied, refused, or failed runs at the reported gate, then create a new run with
        a new reason. Do not retry with the local <code>emisar action run</code>
        command — it bypasses the control plane's policy and approvals.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A dispatch that is refused, or never reaches the runner at all —
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting" <> "#dispatch"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Troubleshooting</.link>
        walks the checks in order.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
